---
import GuideLayout from "@layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const title = "What Is Diminished Value? | FairClaimCalculator";
const description =
  "Diminished value is the drop in a repaired vehicle's resale value from having an accident on its history, even after a quality repair.";
const path = "/guides/diminished-value/what-is-diminished-value/";
---
<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  h1="What Is Diminished Value?"
  cluster={{ name: "Diminished Value", href: "/guides/diminished-value/" }}
  publishedDate="2026-07-29"
  reviewedDate="2026-07-29"
  summary="Diminished value (DV) is the idea that a vehicle is worth less after being in an accident and repaired — even if the repair is perfect — simply because buyers pay less for a car with an accident on its record."
  sources={[
    "General concept explanation; the specific 17c-style formula and its origin are covered in a separate guide with its own primary sources — see below.",
  ]}
  relatedLinks={[
    { label: "Diminished Value Baseline calculator", href: "/calculators/diminished-value-baseline/" },
    { label: "Inherent vs. Repair-Related Diminished Value", href: "/guides/diminished-value/inherent-vs-repair-related-diminished-value/" },
    { label: "The 17c Formula's Real History, Calculation, and Limits", href: "/guides/diminished-value/the-17c-formula-history-calculation-and-limits/" },
    { label: "Does Your State Allow a First-Party Diminished Value Claim?", href: "/guides/diminished-value/state-laws-first-party-diminished-value-claims/" },
  ]}
>
  <h2>The basic idea</h2>
  <p>
    Two identical cars, same year/mileage/trim — except one has a clean title history and one has
    an accident and repair on its vehicle-history report (Carfax, AutoCheck, etc.). Buyers and
    dealers routinely pay less for the second one, even when the repair was done perfectly. That
    gap is diminished value.
  </p>

  <h2>Two different kinds of DV</h2>
  <p>
    "Diminished value" gets used loosely, but it actually describes two different situations — see
    <a href="/guides/diminished-value/inherent-vs-repair-related-diminished-value/">Inherent vs.
    Repair-Related Diminished Value</a> for the full distinction. In short: <strong>inherent DV</strong>
    is the value lost just from having an accident on the record, regardless of repair quality.
    <strong>Repair-related DV</strong> is additional value lost from a repair that was incomplete,
    used non-OEM parts, or wasn't done to the vehicle's original standard.
  </p>

  <h2>Two ways to think about measuring it</h2>
  <p>
    There are two genuinely different approaches, and this site deliberately keeps them
    separate rather than blending them into one number:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A disclosed formula baseline</strong> — a transparent, published calculation (the
      "17c-style" formula) that starts from your car's pre-accident value and applies fixed
      percentage caps and multipliers for damage severity and mileage. See
      <a href="/guides/diminished-value/the-17c-formula-history-calculation-and-limits/">the 17c
      formula guide</a> for its real origin and limits.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Real market evidence</strong> — actual sale prices of comparable clean-title vs.
      accident-history vehicles, which shows what buyers in your specific market actually pay for
      the difference. See
      <a href="/guides/diminished-value/building-diminished-value-market-evidence/">building market
      evidence</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="limits-note">
    <strong>Why we never average these two numbers:</strong> the formula baseline and real market
    evidence measure different things and can disagree significantly. Averaging them would produce
    a number that represents neither — the
    <a href="/calculators/diminished-value-baseline/">Diminished Value Baseline calculator</a> always
    shows both, side by side, never combined.
  </div>

  <h2>What this guide cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    It cannot tell you whether your state recognizes first-party diminished-value claims (some do
    not, or limit them significantly), or what your specific vehicle's diminished value actually is.
    Both depend on your state's law and real market evidence for your car.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
